name: the sweep
after: 30-root
intro: You booted xos onto a box you do not trust -- or you are the defender
intro: making sure it can never be turned against you. An attacker's whole
intro: endgame is two things: PERSISTENCE, surviving a reboot, and EGRESS,
intro: getting a shell or your data back out. This is the sweep. For each
intro: technique you learn the artifact it leaves, the one command that surfaces
intro: it, and why -- on this machine -- most of them cannot arm at all. No
intro: payloads here. A defender never needs to fire the weapon to recognise it.
---
setup: Start with the loudest artifact. A bind shell, or any service an intruder
setup: leaves running, opens a LISTENING socket. On xos there is exactly one
setup: listener you put there: dropbear, bound to the wireguard address alone.
setup: Anything listening on 0.0.0.0 or a public port is not yours.
q: list every listening tcp socket and the program holding it open
a: netstat -tlnp
out: .
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
teach: netstat -l
teach: netstat -n
teach: netstat -p
hint: t tcp, l listening, n numeric ports, p the owning program
---
setup: Now the mirror image. A reverse shell does not listen -- it dials OUT to
setup: the attacker and hands them a prompt. xos is built to dial out too, but to
setup: ONE peer you control, over wireguard. That tiny baseline is the point:
setup: every OTHER established connection is a question you have to answer.
q: show every established tcp connection with the process behind it
a: netstat -tnp
out: .
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
teach: netstat -n
teach: netstat -p
hint: drop -l: not what is waiting, but what is already talking
---
setup: The shortest reverse shells skip the tools entirely and let the SHELL open
setup: the socket, through bash's /dev/tcp. xos ships no bash. /bin/sh is ash and
setup: has no network of its own, so that one-liner has nothing to write to.
q: confirm this shell has no /dev/tcp back door
a: test -e /dev/tcp && echo present || echo absent
out: absent
ref: test
teach: test -e
hint: /dev/tcp was never a file on disk -- it was a bash-only trick
---
setup: A tcp scan misses the quieter channels. A local backdoor can wait on a
setup: unix-domain SOCKET instead of a port -- same idea as the fifo, a file in
setup: the tree a process is listening on, invisible to netstat -t. Sweep those
setup: too. (And note what is NOT a worry here: nc's server mode is compiled out,
setup: so the classic nc -l -e bind shell has no receiving half on this build.)
q: find every unix-domain socket file on the system
a: find / -type s 2>/dev/null
ref: find
teach: find -type
hint: -type s is a socket, the sibling of -type p; account for each one
---
setup: Denied the shell's socket and denied a listener, the last route is a NAMED
setup: PIPE: mkfifo makes a fifo, and a shell reads from it while a client writes
setup: to it. The fifo is the fingerprint -- a pipe sitting in the filesystem,
setup: wired to a running process. So enumerate them and account for every one.
q: find every named pipe (fifo) on the system
a: find / -type p 2>/dev/null
ref: find
teach: find -type
hint: -type p is the fifo; -type s would be a unix socket
---
setup: Persistence next. The quiet backdoor is an extra ssh key: add one line to
setup: authorized_keys and you return whenever you like. On xos that file lives
setup: inside the read-only, verity-covered root -- an attacker cannot append to
setup: it without changing a byte the hash tree covers, which halts the next boot.
setup: The only writable home for a key is the encrypted p3, if you opted in.
q: print every key dropbear is configured to accept
a: cat /state/authorized_keys 2>/dev/null
ref: cat
cmp: ref
teach: cat
hint: on a running stick this is the file to eyeball line by line
---
setup: The oldest persistence of all: a second account with uid 0. root should be
setup: the only name the system answers to as superuser; a hidden second uid-0 is
setup: a whole extra root hiding in plain sight in the passwd database.
q: print every account whose uid is 0
a: awk -F: '$3==0 {print $1}' /etc/passwd
out: root
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
hint: field 3 is the uid; field 1 is the name
---
setup: Attackers love a startup hook -- cron, an init script, a profile file that
setup: runs on every login. xos ships no cron daemon at all, and its init lives on
setup: the read-only root, so there is no writable hook to plant. The way you
setup: confirm it is to look at what is actually running.
q: list every process with its pid, its parent's pid, and its command
a: ps -o pid,ppid,comm
out: .
ref: ps
teach: ps -o
hint: the parent pid is the tell -- a shell whose parent is a network tool
---
setup: A reverse shell, once caught, is a /bin/sh whose PARENT is a network tool
setup: or whose input comes from a socket -- a shell that no login started. The
setup: process TREE makes that parentage obvious at a glance.
q: show the process tree so a shell with the wrong parent stands out
a: pstree
out: .
ref: pstree
teach: pstree
hint: read who spawned whom; a shell under a network tool is the tell
---
setup: Malware often unlinks its own binary after starting, so nothing on disk
setup: points back at it. The kernel still knows: /proc/PID/exe is a link to the
setup: real file, and for a deleted one it reads "(deleted)".
q: show the path of the binary behind this very shell
a: ls -l /proc/$$/exe
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -l
hint: $$ is this shell's pid; a "(deleted)" target is the alarm
---
setup: Egress is not only tcp. Data can leave as dns lookups, icmp, anything the
setup: routes allow. You cannot judge a connection without knowing where traffic
setup: is even permitted to go, so read the map first.
q: show the routing table and the default route
a: ip route
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: one default route, to the network you plugged into -- know it cold
---
setup: Time to turn from hunting to proving. The deepest defense is that the root
setup: filesystem is dm-verity and mounted read-only: an attacker cannot write a
setup: backdoor to it AT ALL, and a single altered byte halts the next boot rather
setup: than booting compromised. Confirm the root is read-only from where you sit.
q: show the root mount's options and pick out that it is read-only
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $4}' /proc/mounts | grep -o ro
ref: grep
cmp: ref
teach: grep -o
hint: field 4 is the mount options; ro is the one that matters
---
setup: A kernel-level implant is the way past a read-only root -- load a module, or
setup: write kernel memory through /dev/mem. xos compiles both out: no module
setup: loader, no /dev/mem. Show that the loader interface is simply not there.
q: report whether the module loader is present or absent
a: test -e /proc/modules && echo present || echo absent
ref: test
cmp: ref
teach: test -e
hint: on xos this reads absent -- there is nothing to load a rootkit into
---
setup: The lockdown state is the kernel's own promise that even root cannot reach
setup: into it -- no /dev/mem, no kexec, no loading unsigned code. Read it straight
setup: from the kernel.
q: print the kernel lockdown state
a: cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown
ref: cat
cmp: ref
teach: cat
hint: the bracketed word is the active level; you want it locked down
---
setup: Last. You have swept for listeners, egress, fifos, keys, hidden roots,
setup: startup hooks and implants, and proven the root cannot be written and the
setup: kernel cannot be reached. One line to state the verdict: the machine is
setup: running the code you signed, and nothing is listening for anyone else.
q: show the root device with its options, and that nothing is waiting on the network
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $1, $4}' /proc/mounts && netstat -tln
out: .
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: two facts in one breath -- the root is yours, the ports are quiet
